IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `cda-nouns`

-- Criminal Damage Act 1971, sections 11(1), 11(11) and 12(1), (3), (6): WHEN THE ACT BEGINS,
-- ARSON AT COMMON LAW, REPEALED PROCEDURE, AND EXTENT.
-- Layer 1 (the law, objectively). Written from the text alone.
--
-- What is encoded from sections 11 and 12 is only what answers a question about a
-- person's own position: from when the Act applies (s 12(1)), whether the common law
-- offence of arson can still be charged (s 11(1)), whether old procedural enactments
-- still apply to an earlier offence (s 11(11)), and where the Act's provisions reach
-- (s 12(3) and (6)).
-- The short title (s 12(2)), the amendment of other enactments (s 11(6), (7)), the repeals
-- (s 11(8) and the Schedule) and the Secretary of State's order-making power (s 11(9),
-- (10)) are machinery and are not encoded; NOTES.md says why for each.
-- Section 7(1) is not here: it is blank in this text. Section 11(2) to (5) and section 12(4)
-- and (5) are blank in this text (dots only).

§ `Section 12(1) -- When the Act comes into force`

-- The date the Act was passed. Section 12(1) says "the day in which it is passed"; the
-- text gives one date, in square brackets under the long title. This module reads that
-- bracketed date as the day the Act was passed (fork F7 in NOTES.md).
-- src:4 | [14th July 1971]
@ref date of enactment (src:4)
GIVETH A DATE
`the day the Act was passed` MEANS Date 14 7 1971

-- "At the expiration of the period of three months beginning with the day in which it is passed":
-- the period begins on 14 July 1971 and runs three months, to the end of 13 October 1971, so
-- the Act is in force from the start of 14 October 1971. `add months` (from daydate) adds the
-- three months to the day it was passed, which is the day after the period ends (fork F7).
-- src:77 | (1) This Act shall come into force at the expiration of the period of three months beginning with the day in which it is passed.
@ref s 12(1) (src:77)
GIVETH A DATE
`the first day the Act is in force` MEANS `add months` `the day the Act was passed` 3

@ref s 12(1) (src:77)
GIVEN day IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the Act is in force on` day MEANS day AT LEAST `the first day the Act is in force`

§ `Section 11(1) -- The common law offence of arson is abolished`

DECLARE `Status of the common law offence of arson` IS ONE OF
    `abolished by section 11(1)`
    `section 11(1) says nothing about an act done before this Act came into force`

-- src:63 | 11 Minor and consequential changes in existing law, and repeals.
-- src:64 | (1) The common law offence of arson is hereby abolished.
@ref s 11(1) (src:63-64)
GIVEN day IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Status of the common law offence of arson`
DECIDE `the status of the common law offence of arson` IS
    IF `the Act is in force on` day
    THEN `abolished by section 11(1)`
    ELSE `section 11(1) says nothing about an act done before this Act came into force`

§ `Section 11(11) -- Repeals of enactments about procedure or jurisdiction`

DECLARE `Effect of the repeal of an enactment about procedure or jurisdiction` IS ONE OF
    `that enactment continues to operate in relation to the offence and to proceedings for it`
    `section 11(11) says nothing about this`

-- src:75 | (11) The repeal by this section or an order made thereunder of any enactment relating to procedure or to the jurisdictional powers of any court shall not affect the operation of that enactment in relation to offences committed before the repeal takes effect or to proceedings for any such offence.
@ref s 11(11) (src:75)
GIVEN `the enactment relates to procedure or to the jurisdictional powers of a court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the enactment is repealed by section 11 or by an order made under it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the offence was committed before the repeal takes effect` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Effect of the repeal of an enactment about procedure or jurisdiction`
DECIDE `the effect of the repeal of an enactment about procedure or jurisdiction` IS
    IF     `the enactment relates to procedure or to the jurisdictional powers of a court`
       AND `the enactment is repealed by section 11 or by an order made under it`
       AND `the offence was committed before the repeal takes effect`
    THEN `that enactment continues to operate in relation to the offence and to proceedings for it`
    ELSE `section 11(11) says nothing about this`

§ `Section 12(3) and (6) -- Extent`

DECLARE Place IS ONE OF
    England
    Wales
    Scotland
    `Northern Ireland`
    `somewhere else`

DECLARE `Whether the provisions of sections 1 to 10 extend to a place` IS ONE OF
    `they extend there`
    `they do not extend there`
    `this text says nothing about that place`

-- The long title says the Act revises "the law of England and Wales as to offences of
-- damage to property". Section 12(3) then says the Act does not extend to Scotland or
-- Northern Ireland "except as provided by subsections (4) to (6)"; subsections (4) and
-- (5) are blank and (6) is about the Schedule (repeals) and section 11(2) and (8), none
-- of which is sections 1 to 10. England and Wales extend by the long title and the
-- contrast, not by an operative sentence: see NOTES.md, fork F9.
-- src:3 | An Act to revise the law of England and Wales as to offences of damage to property, and to repeal or amend as respects the United Kingdom certain enactments relating to such offences; and for connected purposes.
-- src:76 | 12 Short title and extent.
-- src:79 | (3) Except as provided by subsections (4) to (6) below, this Act does not extend to Scotland or Northern Ireland.
-- src:82 | (6) Part II of the Schedule to this Act and so much of section 11(8) above as relates thereto extend to Scotland; and section 11(2) of this Act, Part III of that Schedule and so much of section 11(8) as relates thereto extend to Scotland and Northern Ireland.
@ref long title (src:3)
@ref s 12(3) and (6) (src:76-82)
GIVEN place IS A Place
GIVETH A `Whether the provisions of sections 1 to 10 extend to a place`
DECIDE `whether the provisions of sections 1 to 10 extend to` IS
    CONSIDER place
    WHEN England THEN `they extend there`
    WHEN Wales THEN `they extend there`
    WHEN Scotland THEN `they do not extend there`
    WHEN `Northern Ireland` THEN `they do not extend there`
    WHEN `somewhere else` THEN `this text says nothing about that place`
